{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' <original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,260p' AGENTS.md && sed -n '1,280p' lean/finite_fibers/aristotle_tasks/FF16-full-pauli-support-locality.md && rg --files -g 'HANDOFF*' -g '*STATUS*' -g 'PROGRESS*' -g '*REVIEW*' . ../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization | sort\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' <original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,260p' AGENTS.md && sed -n '1,280p' lean/finite_fibers/aristotle_tasks/FF16-full-pauli-support-locality.md && rg --files -g 'HANDOFF*' -g '*STATUS*' -g 'PROGRESS*' -g '*REVIEW*' . ../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization | sort\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: aristotle-cli\ndescription: Use when Codex should interact with the Aristotle CLI to submit Lean projects or papers, request sorry filling or formalization runs, inspect job status, and download results for iterative project work.\n---\n\n# Aristotle CLI\n\nUse Aristotle for remote Lean/project runs when the local repo is ready but needs automated proof search, sorry filling, or formalization help.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the target project or paper from the current workspace.\n2. Check the local build or project state first. Prefer a narrow local `lake build` or equivalent before submitting anything remote.\n3. Use the bundled wrapper `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh` unless `ARISTOTLE_API_KEY` is already known to be exported in the current process.\n4. Submit the smallest useful task prompt. Keep it concrete and tied to the files at hand.\n5. Wait for the result when that is useful. Otherwise poll or list jobs.\n6. Download artifacts, inspect the diffs, and rerun local verification.\n\n## Commands\n\nUse the commands in [references/aristotle-cli.md](references/aristotle-cli.md).\n\n## Operating rules\n\n- Prefer `aristotle submit` for Lean project work, especially sorry filling or proof search.\n- Prefer `aristotle formalize` when the input is a paper or TeX source that should become a Lean artifact.\n- Use `aristotle list` to inspect recent jobs and `aristotle download` to retrieve results.\n- Keep prompts specific: name the file, theorem, section, or proof obligation instead of asking for broad improvement.\n- If a run fails, inspect the local artifact and re-submit only after the prompt or project state is corrected.\n- Do not print or log the API key.\n- If authentication fails but `~/.bashrc` contains the key, do not assume sourcing `.bashrc` works. Many `.bashrc` files return early for noninteractive shells. Use `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh`.\n\n## When to escalate back to the user\n\n- The CLI is missing, or authentication still fails after trying `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh`.\n- The target repo is not a Lean project or paper and needs clarification.\n- The requested action would overwrite user work outside the target project tree.\n# Local instructions for the finite-fiber Lean package\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis package formalizes the abstract support-corner theorem behind the current\nOverleaf manuscript.  It is intended to be structurally compatible with a\nfuture mathlib contribution.\n\n## Non-negotiable scope\n\n- Use `VonNeumannAlgebra H` and `IsStarProjection` from mathlib.\n- Keep all new definitions in the `FiniteFiber` namespace until an upstream\n  naming/API review identifies a suitable mathlib home.\n- Do not import or copy declarations from `work/OLD/formalization/` merely to\n  make a proof pass.  That project is historical context, not a dependency.\n- Do not add axioms, `sorry`, `admit`, `opaque`, `unsafe`, or\n  `implemented_by` declarations.\n- State every theorem with the exact hypotheses it uses.  In particular,\n  normality and the stabilizer/GNS realization are not silently assumed by the\n  abstract algebraic theorem.\n\n## Dependency order\n\n1. `Basic.lean`: projections, subprojections, Murray--von Neumann equivalence,\n   central projections, finiteness, and semifiniteness.\n2. `TracialCorner.lean`: a complex-linear functional tracial only for pairs of\n   corner elements, and faithful on projections below the support; prove that\n   every subprojection of its support is finite.  Do not strengthen this to a\n   trace on the ambient algebra.\n3. `FiniteFibers.lean`: use fullness to put a nonzero finite projection in\n   every nonzero central summand and specialize to a finite indexed family.\n4. `FiniteStabilizer.lean`: finite self-adjoint group averages are\n   projections, and compatible subgroup actions decrease their supports.\n5. `FiniteStabilizerTrace.lean`: under the explicit nonidentity-tracelessness\n   condition, compute the finite group-average trace and prove nonzero local\n   support in nonzero finite dimension.\n6. `StrongSupport.lean`: construct the canonical strong limit of a decreasing\n   projection sequence and prove its membership in the concrete von Neumann\n   algebra, projection, and fixed-vector consequences.\n7. `StabilizerSequence.lean`: turn an increasing finite-subgroup sequence\n   into the decreasing local support sequence and its common fixed vectors.\n8. `VectorCorner.lean`: derive projection-faithfulness and fullness from a\n   vector functional, dense product orbit, and stated commutation hypotheses.\n9. `GNSVector.lean`: construct the image of the unit for an explicitly\n   supplied positive functional, prove the vector-coefficient and dense-orbit\n   identities, derive unit norm under the explicit normalization condition,\n   and derive fixedness from an explicit zero-deviation condition.\n10. `GNSOrbit.lean`: compose the prior layers into the central-summand theorem.\n   Its corner-traciality field is still an explicit analytic input.\n11. `StabilizerFiniteFiber.lean`: compose finite stabilizer averages, the\n   canonical strong limit, and specified GNS-orbit data into the finite-fiber\n   theorem.\n12. `FinitePauli.lean`, `FiniteQubitMatrix.lean`, `FiniteDensity.lean`, `FinitePauliStabilizer.lean`, and\n    `FiniteLocalPauli.lean`: construct finite Pauli matrices, actual\n    finite-group density matrices, and the expression-level signed partial\n    trace identity.  The expression layer has no hidden group/state hypothesis.\n13. `FinitePauliGNS.lean`: apply the generic GNS-vector result to a finite\n    stabilizer density and prove that each represented finite stabilizer fixes\n    its own GNS vector.\n14. `FiniteNestedPauli.lean`: under an explicit tensor-coordinate\n    decomposition, injective local-group inclusion, and exact survivor iff,\n    identify the expression data with the actual positive trace-one stabilizer\n    densities and prove their partial-trace compatibility.\n15. `FiniteLocalGroupSupport.lean`: from an explicit finite-support map and a\n    discarded-label/support detection condition, construct support-defined\n    local groups, their canonical nested-region inclusion, and the survivor\n    iff used by `FiniteNestedPauli.lean`.\n16. `ProjectivePauliLattice.lean` and `FullPauliLattice.lean`: construct the\n    finite-support normal-form Pauli groups and the algebraic generated\n    consistent stabilizer subgroup.  Do not call this a matrix realization or\n    identify syntactic support with local-algebra membership.\n17. `FiniteTensorMatrixChain.lean`: construct literal initial/final `Fin`\n    tensor coordinates and the trace/partial-trace pairing identity.\n18. `FiniteRegionQubitAlgebra.lean`: construct the finite matrix algebra on\n    each `F : Finset Site`, the canonical `F' = F \\sqcup (F' \\setminus F)`\n    bit-coordinate split, and the reindexed `A \\mapsto A \\otimes 1` inclusion\n    for `F \\subseteq F'`.  Its matrix operator norm is provided through the\n    scoped `FiniteFiber.FiniteRegionQubitMatrix` instance.  Do not identify\n    Pauli syntax or stabilizer support with these algebras yet, or claim\n    multi-region coherence, a colimit, a state, or GNS data.\n19. `FiniteRegionPauliMatrices.lean`: realize only a phase-free Hermitian\n    `PauliLabel` string as an actual matrix on every finite-region basis, with\n    all-identity, self-adjointness, and identity-padding under the checked\n    one-inclusion.  This is not a representation or multiplication theorem for\n    the `i^phase X^x Z^z` normal form: its finite-region `ZMod 4`/`Y` phase\n    aggregation, support/local-algebra membership, and coherence remain\n    separate work.\n20. `OneSitePauliNormalForm.lean`: fix exactly the one-site conversion from\n    the Hermitian `Y` representative to the raw normal-form order,\n    `X Z = i^3 Y = -i Y`.  It is not a finite-region or `FullPauliWord`\n    representation, multiplication theorem, support/locality statement, or\n    coherence result; those require later correction aggregation and cocycle\n    work.\n21. `FiniteNestedTensorState.lean`: construct the one-inclusion finite-density\n    and state restriction in literal finite `Fin` coordinates.\n22. `FiniteStabilizerStateChain.lean`: construct sequential transitions by\n    composing physical one-site maps, prove their typed identity/composition\n    laws, injectivity, and norm preservation, relate endpoint maps to the\n    length-indexed physical recursion, and lift adjacent density compatibility\n    to every finite sequential transition.\n23. `SequentialLocalFunctional.lean`: form the associated quotient module and\n    descend compatible finite functionals to a linear functional.  The\n    component maps are a coherent directed system of unital star-algebra maps,\n    but do not call its module quotient a star-algebra, quasilocal algebra, or\n    state until quotient multiplication, star, positivity, and completion are\n    proved.\n24. `CountablePauliRepresentation.lean`: construct the discrete countable\n    binary configuration Hilbert space and its bounded translation/phase\n    generators, together with their almost-everywhere action formulas.  Do not\n    call this an actual finite local matrix-algebra realization before a\n    finite/tail factorization and the operator relations are checked.\n25. A later layer must identify the finite-region matrix algebras with the\n    full phased Pauli/stabilizer realization and support/local-algebra\n    membership, then construct a coherent quasilocal algebra and global state,\n    then construct the GNS-orbit/trace hypotheses and any direct-integral/type\n    conclusion.  Do not represent that work as completed here.\n\n## Verification\n\nRun the narrow Lake target checks before `lake build`, including\n`lake build FiniteFiber.Basic`, `lake build FiniteFiber.TracialCorner`,\n`lake build FiniteFiber.FiniteFibers`, `lake build FiniteFiber.FiniteStabilizer`,\n`lake build FiniteFiber.FiniteStabilizerTrace`, `lake build FiniteFiber.StrongSupport`, `lake build FiniteFiber.StabilizerSequence`,\n`lake build FiniteFiber.VectorCorner`, `lake build FiniteFiber.GNSVector`, `lake build FiniteFiber.GNSOrbit`, and\n`lake build FiniteFiber.StabilizerFiniteFiber`, `lake build\nFiniteFiber.FinitePauli`, `lake build FiniteFiber.FiniteQubitMatrix`, `lake build\nFiniteFiber.FiniteDensity`, `lake build FiniteFiber.FinitePauliStabilizer`, and `lake build\nFiniteFiber.FinitePauliGNS`, `lake build FiniteFiber.FiniteLocalPauli`, `lake build FiniteFiber.FiniteNestedPauli`, and\n`lake build FiniteFiber.FiniteLocalGroupSupport`, `lake build\nFiniteFiber.ProjectivePauliLattice`, `lake build FiniteFiber.FullPauliLattice`,\n`lake build FiniteFiber.OneSitePauliNormalForm`,\n`lake build FiniteFiber.FiniteTensorMatrixChain`, `lake build\nFiniteFiber.FiniteRegionQubitAlgebra`, `lake build\nFiniteFiber.FiniteRegionPauliMatrices`, `lake build\nFiniteFiber.FiniteNestedTensorState`, and `lake build\nFiniteFiber.FiniteStabilizerStateChain`, and `lake build\nFiniteFiber.SequentialLocalFunctional`, and `lake build\nFiniteFiber.CountablePauliRepresentation`.\nCheck that the target files contain no placeholders and inspect `#print axioms`\nfor each public theorem.  Compile the matching TeX records in\n`../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I001-full-tracial-corner/`\n`../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I002-stabilizer-gns-realization/`,\nand `../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I003-finite-pauli-density-realization/`.\n# FF16 — full-Pauli support and finite-region locality\n\nRead `manuscript/main.tex`, especially the short-range basis and local-algebra\ndiscussion. Work only in `formalization_finite_fibers`.\n\nCreate only `FiniteFiber/FullPauliSupportLocality.lean`. Build on FF15,\n`FullPauliLattice`, `FiniteRegionQubitAlgebra`, and\n`FiniteRegionFullPauliMultiplication`. For a `FullPauliWord Site` whose label\nsupport lies in a finite region `F`, construct its finite-region matrix and\nprove that enlarging `F` pads it by the identity through `finiteRegionEmbed`.\nState and prove the exact equivalence between the word's explicit finite\nsupport predicate and availability of this local matrix realization; do not\nidentify it with membership in an unspecified global algebra.\n\nThis is finite infrastructure only. Do not construct a quasilocal completion,\nstate, GNS representation, normality, or a von Neumann algebra. No `sorry`,\n`admit`, axiom, `opaque`, `unsafe`, or `native_decide`. If an exact support\nequivalence is unavailable, return the strongest compiling one-way locality\ntheorem and name the missing finite-support lemma.\n\nAcceptance: `lake build FiniteFiber.FullPauliSupportLocality` succeeds and the\nresponse lists every supplied finite-region hypothesis.\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/HANDOFF.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF01-extracted/formalization_finite_fibers_aristotle/HANDOFF.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF02-extracted/project_aristotle/HANDOFF.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF03-extracted/project_aristotle/HANDOFF.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF04-extracted/project_aristotle/HANDOFF.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF05-extracted/project_aristotle/HANDOFF.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF06-extracted/project_aristotle/HANDOFF.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF07-extracted/project_aristotle/HANDOFF.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF08-extracted/formalization_finite_fibers_aristotle/HANDOFF.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF10-extracted/formalization_finite_fibers_aristotle/HANDOFF.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF11-extracted/formalization_finite_fibers_aristotle/HANDOFF.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF12-extracted/formalization_finite_fibers_aristotle/HANDOFF.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF13-extracted/formalization_finite_fibers_aristotle/HANDOFF.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF14-extracted/formalization_finite_fibers_aristotle/HANDOFF.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF15-extracted/aristotle-finite_fiber-38r6u_k1_aristotle/HANDOFF.md\n./HANDOFF.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' <original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/references/aristotle-cli.md && sed -n '1,300p' ../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/HANDOFF.md && find ../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization -maxdepth 2 -type f \\\\( -iname '*status*' -o -iname '*handoff*' -o -iname '*review*' \\\\) -print | sort && sed -n '1,160p' ../work/aristotle_autopilot/logs/20260806T183321Z-fdc7bd0f-tasks.log && ls -la FiniteFiber | tail -30 && rg -n \\\"FullPauliSupport|FiniteRegionFullPauli|finiteRegion.*FullPauli|FullPauliWord\\\" FiniteFiber lakefile.lean\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' <original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/references/aristotle-cli.md && sed -n '1,300p' ../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/HANDOFF.md && find ../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization -maxdepth 2 -type f \\\\( -iname '*status*' -o -iname '*handoff*' -o -iname '*review*' \\\\) -print | sort && sed -n '1,160p' ../work/aristotle_autopilot/logs/20260806T183321Z-fdc7bd0f-tasks.log && ls -la FiniteFiber | tail -30 && rg -n \\\"FullPauliSupport|FiniteRegionFullPauli|finiteRegion.*FullPauli|FullPauliWord\\\" FiniteFiber lakefile.lean\"","aggregated_output":"# Aristotle CLI reference\n\n## Setup\n\n- Install: `uv tool install aristotlelib`\n- Verify: `aristotle --version`\n- Authenticate: set `ARISTOTLE_API_KEY` in the shell environment, or use the skill wrapper `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh`.\n\nIf the key is stored near the end of `~/.bashrc`, noninteractive commands may not see it because many `.bashrc` files contain:\n\n```bash\ncase $- in\n    *i*) ;;\n      *) return;;\nesac\n```\n\nIn that case, do not rely on `source ~/.bashrc`. Use the wrapper, which reads a literal `export ARISTOTLE_API_KEY=...` assignment without printing it:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh formalize input.md\n```\n\n## Core commands\n\nSubmit a Lean project for sorry filling or proof search:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh submit \"Fill in all sorries\" --project-dir ./my-lean-project --wait\n```\n\nFormalize a TeX or paper source:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh formalize paper.tex --wait --destination output.tar.gz\n```\n\nList recent jobs:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh list --limit 10\n```\n\nDownload a completed job:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh download <project-id> --destination result.tar.gz\n```\n\nPass the key directly for one command only:\n\n```bash\naristotle submit \"My prompt\" --api-key \"$ARISTOTLE_API_KEY\" --project-dir ./my-lean-project --wait\n```\n\n## Recommended project loop\n\n1. Inspect the Lean project locally.\n2. Run the narrowest local build or test that exercises the target files.\n3. Submit a focused Aristotle prompt against the project directory.\n4. Wait for the run if the expected result is a concrete patch.\n5. Download or inspect the produced artifact.\n6. Rebuild locally and repeat with a tighter prompt if needed.\n\n## Prompt shape\n\nKeep prompts short and file-bound. Good prompts mention:\n\n- the exact project directory\n- the theorem or file to work on\n- whether to fill `sorry`s, formalize a statement, or debug a failing proof\n- what counts as a useful partial result\n\nAvoid vague prompts such as \"improve this project\" unless the scope is intentionally broad.\n# Handoff: I004 countable Pauli realization and quasi-local algebra\n\n## Resume point\n\nI004 has begun.  Its canonical scope and limits are in `QUESTION.tex` and\n`technical.tex`, both compiled successfully on August 3, 2026.  The new Lean\nfoundation is `formalization_finite_fibers/FiniteFiber/CountablePauliRepresentation.lean`.\n\nThe global finite-fiber theorem is **not** instantiated yet.  The current\nmodule only makes the first concrete countable representation ingredients\navailable.\n\n## What is checked now\n\nFor the countable finite-support binary configuration group\n\n```text\nK = ℕ →₀ ZMod 2,\nH = Lp ℂ 2 (Measure.count : Measure K),\n```\n\nthe package now has a discrete measurable-space instance and constructs:\n\n- `configurationBit i : K`, nonzero and of additive order two;\n- `configurationInitial n` and `configurationTail n`, the additive filters to\n  sites below and at-or-beyond the cut, respectively;\n- their recomposition theorem, their complementary coordinate-vanishing\n  theorems, and their action on a one-site configuration on the appropriate\n  side of the cut;\n- `configurationShift g : H →L[ℂ] H`, induced by count-measure-preserving\n  translation `x ↦ g + x`;\n- `configurationShift_zero`, proving that neutral translation is the identity;\n- `configurationShift_add`, proving\n  `configurationShift (g + h) = (configurationShift h).comp (configurationShift g)`;\n- `countablePauliX i`, the shift by `configurationBit i`;\n- `countablePauliX_comm`, proving that two such `X` translations commute;\n- `countablePauliX_comp_self`, proving that each one-site `X` generator is an\n  involution;\n- `phaseCharacter i : K → ℂ`, with square one and the correct same-site sign\n  flip / distinct-site invariance identities;\n- `configurationPhase i : H →L[ℂ] H`, obtained by bounded `L∞` multiplication;\n- `configurationPhase_comp_self`, proving that this phase operator squares to\n  the identity; and\n- `countablePauliZ i`, the corresponding local phase operator;\n- `countablePauliZ_comm`, proving that two `Z` generators commute;\n- `countablePauliZ_comp_countablePauliX_same`, proving same-site\n  anticommutation; and\n- `countablePauliZ_comp_countablePauliX_other`, proving mixed commutation at\n  distinct sites.\n\nThe checked action formulas are almost-everywhere formulas:\n\n```text\nconfigurationShift g f (x) = f (g + x),\nconfigurationPhase i f (x) = phaseCharacter i x * f x,\n```\n\nThe translation map preserves the `L²` norm exactly, has the checked\nzero-shift identity, and obeys the checked composition law above.  Thus the\nmodule now has the complete elementary `X`/`Z` generator relations, with all\nequalities of `Lp` representatives proved through their almost-everywhere\naction formulas.  At the configuration-group level it also has the checked\nadditive initial/tail split; this is a prerequisite for, but is not itself,\nthe finite/tail `L²` factorization.\n\n## What is deliberately not proved\n\nDo not promote this module to a countable Pauli matrix realization yet.  It\ndoes **not** currently prove:\n\n1. a full phased finite-support Pauli representation;\n2. the finite/tail Hilbert-space factorization and the identification of a\n   finite-site Pauli image with `M_(2^n)(ℂ) ⊗ 1`;\n3. a concrete increasing family of finite local star subalgebras;\n4. the norm-closed quasi-local C-star algebra;\n5. extension of compatible finite stabilizer functionals to a positive,\n   normalized state on that closure;\n6. the actual stabilizer-locality, GNS regional product-orbit, and\n   corner-traciality hypotheses needed to instantiate I002/I001.\n\nThus the manuscript theorem\n`thm:finite_projections_exist` remains only conditionally formalized through\nthe previously checked support-corner theorem.\n\n## Exact next Lean task\n\nFF01 promoted the shift-composition theorem on August 4, 2026.  The reviewed\ncandidate uses `MeasurePreserving.quasiMeasurePreserving.ae_eq_comp` to pull\nthe shift formula back along a translation and normalizes the composed map\nwith `ContinuousLinearMap.comp_apply`; it is now permanent source, not a\ntemporary MWE.\n\nFF02 promoted the zero-shift identity, FF03 phase involution, and FF04 the\ncommutation of the `X` translations.  FF05--FF07 complete the remaining\nelementary bounded-operator relations, and FF08 completes the additive\ninitial/tail split of `CountablePauliConfiguration`.  FF09 was explicitly set\naside as irrelevant to the immediate finite-fiber proof route.\n\nThe direct finite-region bridge starts with accepted FF10\n(`FiniteRegionQubitAlgebra`), FF11 (`FiniteRegionPauliMatrices`), and now\nFF12 (`OneSitePauliNormalForm`).  FF12 fixes the one-site phase convention\nneeded to turn the Hermitian `Y` representative into the raw normal form\n`X Z = -iY`, with a `ZMod 4` correction of `3` at `Y`.  It neither builds a\nfinite-region/full-word map nor proves group multiplication.  The next bridge\nis a finite-region correction sum and padding formula, followed by the\nraw-cocycle/multiplication compatibility needed before any local-group\nrepresentation claim.  Any later countable operator construction must still\nkeep equality of `Lp` representatives almost-everywhere where applicable.\n\nFF02--FF04 were accepted and integrated on August 5, 2026: FF02\n(`f5883a1c-9605-4e52-aac0-c0ee8b5a928c`), FF03\n(`e18a3e80-c3c2-4f23-a320-ebfd9ac92cf9`), and FF04\n(`d9f4dcfc-d8a3-44a4-bc37-bb0aaf367c9e`).  Each returned snapshot is kept in\nits own `aristotle_responses/FF0*-extracted/` quarantine directory.  All three\nsnapshots contained the same unrelated README attribution insertion; it was\nrejected, and only the reviewed candidate Lean declarations were integrated.\nThe approved final reviews are\n`aristotle_responses/FF02-integration-adversarial-review.md`,\n`aristotle_responses/FF03-integration-adversarial-review.md`, and\n`aristotle_responses/FF04-integration-adversarial-review.md`.\n\nFF05--FF07 were submitted in parallel and accepted on August 5, 2026: FF05 (one-site\n`X` involution), project `ea714466-c37d-41f0-8b86-7c4906330d56`; FF06\n(commutation of the `Z` phase generators), project\n`a8f2fb66-8bc7-41e3-be24-16aa5f57c192`; and FF07 (same-site mixed\nanticommutation and distinct-site mixed commutation), project\n`c280ca7f-3f19-40e9-9bd3-fd3a4e718cc5`.  Each returned snapshot is quarantined\nin its `aristotle_responses/FF0*-extracted/` directory.  As with FF02--FF04,\nevery snapshot contained an unrelated README attribution insertion; it was\nrejected, and only the reviewed candidate Lean declarations were integrated.\nTheir independent prompt audits are approved at\n`aristotle_responses/FF05-prompt-adversarial-review.md`,\n`aristotle_responses/FF06-prompt-adversarial-review.md`, and\n`aristotle_responses/FF07-prompt-adversarial-review.md`; the approved final\nintegration reviews are\n`aristotle_responses/FF05-integration-adversarial-review.md`,\n`aristotle_responses/FF06-integration-adversarial-review.md`, and\n`aristotle_responses/FF07-integration-adversarial-review.md`.\n\nFF08, the additive initial/tail split of\n`CountablePauliConfiguration`, was accepted and integrated on August 5, 2026\nfrom project `5a41c5b2-24ef-4f49-9d6f-03e203a80ff3`.  Its archive\n`aristotle_responses/FF08-initial-tail-configuration-split.tar.gz` has SHA-256\n`a9355ae84e5a093aadfac197565a9376ad776f375028696f4bf0ebcf32716793` and is\nquarantined at `aristotle_responses/FF08-extracted/`.  Only\n`AristotleCandidates/InitialTailConfigurationSplit.lean` was integrated; the\nremote README attribution, stale HANDOFF deletion, and metadata were rejected.\nThe prompt audit and approved post-result review are\n`aristotle_responses/FF08-prompt-adversarial-review.md` and\n`aristotle_responses/FF08-integration-adversarial-review.md`.  Its scope is\nexactly the `Finsupp.filter` decomposition and its one-site consequences; it\ndoes not assert the needed Hilbert-space factorization or a local matrix\nalgebra.\n\nFF09, the additive equivalence between a configuration and its FF08\ninitial/tail components, was submitted to Aristotle on August 5, 2026 as\nproject `f78a4014-b751-45d0-af0a-b1e8a9e32c11`.  Its prompt audit is approved\nat `aristotle_responses/FF09-prompt-adversarial-review.md`.  It remains\nremote-only: no result has been retrieved or integrated.  It is constrained\nto an additive-subgroup equivalence of configurations and does not assert an\n`L²` factorization, finite-coordinate equivalence, matrix algebra, or GNS\nstatement.\n\n## Current bridge after FF09\n\nFF09 was explicitly set aside as irrelevant to the immediate finite-fiber\nproof route.  It remains remote-only and is not to be retrieved or integrated.\n\nFF10, project `88d5f994-e829-4ded-b04a-b38c650e4f8b`, was accepted and\nintegrated on August 5, 2026.  Its remote archive has SHA-256\n`7ca2f97893a1fc872626a0a7095b14887504c36939aa3b1e584cf086b0fa5778` and is\nquarantined at `aristotle_responses/FF10-extracted/`.  Only its candidate was\nintegrated as `formalization_finite_fibers/FiniteFiber/FiniteRegionQubitAlgebra.lean`.\nFor arbitrary `F \\subseteq F' : Finset Site`, it gives the finite-region\nqubit basis, the corresponding finite matrix algebra, a canonical basis split\ninto `F` and `F' \\ F`, and the reindexed `A \\mapsto A \\otimes 1` inclusion\nwith its explicit formula, injectivity, and operator-norm preservation.  The\ncandidate's file-local C-star instance was promoted to a named scoped instance\nso that downstream modules can use the norm theorem.  It makes no claim about\nPauli/stabilizer realization, compositional coherence, a local or quasilocal\ncolimit, state/GNS data, or the theorem on finite projections.  Its prompt and\npost-result reviews are `aristotle_responses/FF10-prompt-adversarial-review.md`\nand `aristotle_responses/FF10-integration-adversarial-review.md`.\n\nFF11, project `76d70d89-bf7a-490b-a1ee-6c3512437110`, was accepted and\nintegrated on August 5, 2026.  Its archive\n`aristotle_responses/FF11-finite-region-pauli-matrices.tar.gz` has SHA-256\n`acaaeac3024447b211faf050224b9146e978d90c105da094a22b9226d84b529a`; the\ncomplete remote snapshot is quarantined at `aristotle_responses/FF11-extracted/`.\nOnly the returned candidate was integrated, byte-for-byte, as\n`formalization_finite_fibers/FiniteFiber/FiniteRegionPauliMatrices.lean`.\nThe remote README attribution insertion, stale FF11 handoff deletion,\n`ARISTOTLE_SUMMARY.md`, and unsupported commit/push claim were rejected.\n\nThe checked module realizes phase-free Hermitian Pauli-label strings directly\non arbitrary finite-region bases; it proves the all-identity and self-adjoint\nfacts and exact identity-padding compatibility with `finiteRegionEmbed`.  It\ndoes not represent or multiply `FullPauliWord`: the normal form\n`i^phase X^x Z^z` now has its one-site correction, but still requires\nfinite-region `ZMod 4`/Y-count aggregation.\nIt also makes no support/locality, coherence, colimit, state, GNS, or\nfinite-projection claim.  Both independent reviews approve the integration;\nsee `aristotle_responses/FF11-prompt-adversarial-review.md`,\n`aristotle_responses/FF11-integration-adversarial-review.md`, and\n`aristotle_tasks/FF11-finite-region-pauli-matrices.md`.\n\nFF12, project `c091ae7a-c696-4863-a46f-72c8ab6ca624`, was accepted and\nintegrated on August 5, 2026.  Its archive\n`aristotle_responses/FF12-one-site-pauli-normal-form.tar.gz` has SHA-256\n`624b130a2c08061fa8f60edf33397c5a2dd82b4817569c0acdcd7f14c9da6cbf`; the\ncomplete remote snapshot is quarantined at `aristotle_responses/FF12-extracted/`.\nOnly `AristotleCandidates/OneSitePauliNormalForm.lean` was integrated,\nbyte-for-byte, as `formalization_finite_fibers/FiniteFiber/OneSitePauliNormalForm.lean`\n(source SHA-256\n`6d9e4ae4f3c0ae3a3c3b9d35322d42ddd8d518a07f75441b6b1a61a3984cfc99`).  The\nremote README attribution insertion, stale FF12 handoff deletion, and\n`ARISTOTLE_SUMMARY.md` were rejected.\n\nThe checked module proves exactly `X Z = i^3Y = -iY` and the corresponding\nnonadditive correction `3` at the projective `Y` coordinate.  It does not\nreuse the sign map `phaseEmbed 1 = 2`, nor does it make a finite-region,\nfull-word, cocycle, local-group, quasilocal, GNS, or finite-projection claim.\nIts prompt and post-result reviews are\n`aristotle_responses/FF12-prompt-adversarial-review.md` and\n`aristotle_responses/FF12-integration-adversarial-review.md`.\n\n## Verification record\n\nPassed after integrating FF01--FF08:\n\n```bash\ncd formalization_finite_fibers\nlake env lean FiniteFiber/CountablePauliRepresentation.lean\nlake build FiniteFiber.CountablePauliRepresentation\nlake build FiniteFiber\nlake build\nlake env lean tests/AxiomAudit.lean\n```\n\nThe public-theorem audit now includes `configurationShift_zero`,\n`countablePauliX_comm`, `configurationPhase_comp_self`,\n`countablePauliX_comp_self`, `countablePauliZ_comm`,\n`countablePauliZ_comp_countablePauliX_same`, and\n`countablePauliZ_comp_countablePauliX_other`,\n`configurationInitial_add_configurationTail`,\n`configurationInitial_apply_of_le`, `configurationTail_apply_of_lt`,\n`configurationInitial_bit_of_lt`, and `configurationTail_bit_of_le`.  Their\nexpected axioms remain only `propext`, `Classical.choice`, and `Quot.sound`;\nno project-local axiom, `sorry`, `admit`, `opaque`, `unsafe`, or\n`implemented_by` declaration is present in the new module.\n\nFF11 was verified after integration with:\n\n```bash\ncd formalization_finite_fibers\nlake env lean FiniteFiber/FiniteRegionPauliMatrices.lean\nlake build FiniteFiber.FiniteRegionPauliMatrices\nlake build FiniteFiber\nlake env lean tests/AxiomAudit.lean\nlake build\n```\n\nAll passed.  The three FF11 public theorems have only `propext`,\n`Classical.choice`, and `Quot.sound` in their axiom footprints.\n\nFF12 was verified after integration with:\n\n~~~bash\ncd formalization_finite_fibers\nlake env lean FiniteFiber/OneSitePauliNormalForm.lean\nlake build FiniteFiber.OneSitePauliNormalForm\nlake build FiniteFiber\nlake env lean tests/AxiomAudit.lean\nlake build\n~~~\n\nAll passed.  The three FF12 public theorems have only `propext`,\n`Classical.choice`, and `Quot.sound` in their axiom footprints.\n\nThe I004 TeX records were built with:\n\n```bash\nlatexmk -pdf -interaction=nonstopmode QUESTION.tex\nlatexmk -pdf -interaction=nonstopmode technical.tex\n```\n\n`QUESTION.pdf` has one page and `technical.pdf` has three pages.  Their final\nlog audit has no undefined references, errors, or layout diagnostics.\n\nIndependent adversarial reviews approve this exact bounded-operator scope:\n\n- `reviews/math_fidelity_review.md`;\n- `reviews/lean_api_review.md`.\n- `aristotle_responses/FF01-adversarial-review.md` approves the promoted\n  shift-composition theorem.  The remote archive's unrelated README insertion\n  was rejected and not integrated.\n- `aristotle_responses/FF02-integration-adversarial-review.md`,\n  `aristotle_responses/FF03-integration-adversarial-review.md`, and\n  `aristotle_responses/FF04-integration-adversarial-review.md` approve the\n  three subsequent bounded-operator promotions and the rejection of every\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/HANDOFF.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF01-adversarial-review.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF01-integration-adversarial-review.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF02-FF04-live-closeout-adversarial-review.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF02-integration-adversarial-review.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF02-prompt-adversarial-review.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF03-integration-adversarial-review.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF03-prompt-adversarial-review.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF04-integration-adversarial-review.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von-neumann-formalization/investigations/I004-countable-pauli-quasilocal-realization/aristotle_responses/FF04-prompt-adversarial-review.md\n../work/2026-08-finite-fiber-von